{
"A century later the new system was better by every measure.": "307ad03542ca",
"A revolution, for Kuhn, is the replacement of one paradigm by another, and it does not happen the way a theorem is proved. The two sides do not share enough to settle the matter by a single experiment. Copernicuss system was at first no more accurate than Ptolemys. It still used epicycles, since Copernicus kept the old belief that heavenly motion must be circular. It also contradicted the best physics of the day, which said that a moving earth would leave falling stones and clouds behind. What it offered was a kind of order. In Ptolemys system every planets epicycle was mysteriously tied to the suns yearly motion. Copernicus explained this at a stroke: the yearly circle is ours. The backward loop of Mars is what we see when the faster earth overtakes it.": "b44cd6b57abc",
"An official brought charges against him.": "b4b68c707d4b",
"Anomalies pile up slowly.": "e4cdb831fea1",
"Astronomy was bound up with institutions and loyalties.": "9f1d2d830e19",
"Buddhism has had to face a change of this kind. The traditional texts describe a flat world with a great mountain, Meru, at its center, four continents around it, and the sun and moon circling its peak. For two thousand years this was the cosmos of Buddhist Asia, taught in monasteries and painted on walls. It is not the cosmos of modern astronomy. Buddhists have met the difficulty in several ways, and the plainest was stated by the Dalai Lama: if scientific analysis were conclusively to show that certain claims in Buddhism are false, then Buddhists must accept the findings of science and abandon those claims. He has applied this to Mount Meru.": "99ce28f7decc",
"China had its own astronomy, with careful records of eclipses, comets, and guest stars going back two thousand years, and its own rival pictures of the sky. In the 1600s Jesuit missionaries brought European methods to the court in Beijing and showed that they predicted eclipses more accurately. In 1644 the German Jesuit Adam Schall was put in charge of the imperial calendar. Twenty years later an official named Yang Guangxian brought charges against him and the Chinese astronomers who worked with him. Yangs objection was not mainly about accuracy. He is remembered for the sentence: It is better for China to have no good calendar than to have Westerners in China. Schall was condemned, and five Chinese astronomers were executed. Yang took over the bureau, his predictions failed, and in 1669 a public contest of predictions restored the Jesuits methods.": "14b2708b87ca",
"Copernicus explained the coincidence at a stroke.": "88d24af40f91",
"For a long time the anomalies are set aside.": "829458875cff",
"For it is not unknown that Lactantius, otherwise a famous writer but a poor mathematician, speaks most childishly of the shape of the Earth when he makes fun of those who said that the Earth has the form of a sphere. It should not seem strange then to zealous students, if some such people shall ridicule us also. Mathematics are written for mathematicians.": "c61f956876ab",
"He had kept the manuscript back for decades.": "c13c92b47a7d",
"I can easily conceive, most Holy Father, that as soon as some people learn that in this book which I have written concerning the revolutions of the heavenly bodies, I ascribe certain motions to the Earth, they will cry out at once that I and my theory should be rejected. For I am not so much in love with my conclusions as not to weigh what others will think about them, and although I know that the meditations of a philosopher are far removed from the judgment of the laity, because his endeavor is to seek out the truth in all things, so far as this is permitted by God to the human reason, I still believe that one must avoid theories altogether foreign to orthodoxy.": "225f09740110",
"In 1962 the historian Thomas Kuhn asked how such changes come about, and gave an answer that surprised philosophers. Most science, he said, is not a search for new theories. It is work done inside a paradigm, by which he meant a shared achievement that tells a community what the world contains, which questions are worth asking, and what a good answer looks like. Ptolemys astronomy was a paradigm, and Newtons mechanics was another. Within one, scientists do what Kuhn called normal science. They solve puzzles, measuring a constant more exactly or extending the theory to a new case. They do not test the paradigm. They assume it, and when an experiment disagrees with it they suspect the experiment.": "cf6ac3cd7dfc",
"It is a reason, and it falls short of a proof.": "861d8b16b1c5",
"It took more than a century, and the work of Kepler, Galileo, and Newton, before the new system was plainly better by every measure. During that time, Kuhn argued, choosing it was a judgment that the evidence did not force. He compared the change to the drawing that can be seen as a duck or as a rabbit. The lines are the same, and what one sees in them has shifted. Scientists on either side of a revolution, he wrote, work in different worlds. The physicist Max Planck had said something harsher from his own experience: A new scientific truth does not triumph by convincing its opponents and making them see the light, but rather because its opponents eventually die, and a new generation grows up that is familiar with it.": "c15aa1f9ab54",
"Kuhn thought this conservatism necessary. Only people who trust a framework will push it far enough to find where it breaks. Results that will not fit, which he called anomalies, pile up slowly. For a long time they are set aside. When there are too many of them, or one of them touches something central, the field enters a crisis. Rival ideas appear, and the young begin to take them seriously.": "ada47cf170ab",
"Kuhns book made many readers conclude that science is a succession of fashions and that no paradigm is truer than another. He denied this for the rest of his life. Later theories, he said, are better at solving puzzles than earlier ones: more accurate, wider in scope, simpler, and more fruitful of new results. These values are shared across revolutions, and they are why science does not go round in circles. What he would not say is that later theories are closer to the truth about what the world is really like, since he saw no way to compare a theory with the world except through another theory.": "9a13fb111767",
"One needs a reason that bears on that result.": "d01814f5f49c",
"That last step is still argued over. Realists point out that theories which survive a revolution usually keep the successes of the old ones as special cases, as Einsteins equations give Newtons at low speeds, and that this looks like getting nearer to something. Their opponents reply with history: most past theories that were once successful are now thought false, so we should expect the same of ours. Both sides accept that science changes its mind. It does so rarely, slowly, and under pressure, and when it has changed it can do more than it could before.": "8e398f53822a",
"That part of his account has held up well.": "ecfa68e35056",
"The early discourses give a reason why this can be done without loss. A monk once told the Buddha that he would leave the order unless he was told whether the cosmos is eternal or not, finite or infinite. The Buddha replied with the simile of a man struck by a poisoned arrow who refuses to have it removed until he knows who shot it, of what clan, with what kind of bow. He would die first. I have not declared these things, the Buddha said, and what he had declared was suffering, its origin, its ending, and the path. The cosmology in the texts was the common knowledge of ancient India, used as a setting. It was never the teaching. In Kuhns terms the Buddha asked his followers to keep the puzzle and hold the paradigm loosely, which is what a tradition must do if it is to outlive the science of its time (Dharma and Western Philosophy D18).": "2b11e8886c56",
"The episode shows both of Kuhns points and one that tells against him. A way of doing astronomy was bound up with institutions, loyalties, and a view of the world, and it was defended on those grounds. Yet the two sides could agree on a test, the length of a shadow at noon on a named day, and the result decided the matter for the emperor. Paradigms may be different worlds. They can still be made to meet at particular points.": "a2e71953221d",
"This was good science. It was mathematical, it was tested against observation, and it was improved by generations of astronomers in Greek, Arabic, and Latin. In 1543 Nicolaus Copernicus published a book that put the sun at the center and set the earth moving. He knew what to expect. I can easily conceive, most Holy Father, he wrote to the pope, that as soon as some people learn that in this book I ascribe certain motions to the Earth, they will cry out at once that I and my theory should be rejected. He had kept the manuscript back for decades.": "f1b814527254",
"Watch Mars for a few months and you will see something odd. Night after night it drifts eastward against the stars. Then it slows, stops, moves backward for several weeks, stops again, and goes on as before. Every planet does this. Around 150 CE the astronomer Ptolemy of Alexandria gave an explanation that satisfied the learned world for fourteen centuries. The earth stands still at the center. Each planet rides on a small circle, the epicycle, whose center moves around a large one. When the planet is on the inner part of its small circle it appears from the earth to move backward. By choosing the sizes and speeds of the circles Ptolemy could predict where each planet would be on any night, well enough for calendars, navigation, and horoscopes.": "6e090b19814b",
